# Saved natal persistence: approved bounded implementation contract

Implement an optional profile-domain persistence primitive after
`computePortableChart`, in `src/lib/profile/saved-record.ts` and
`src/lib/profile/saved-record-store.ts`. The accountable integrator approved this
bounded direction and assigned a separate author. This review writes no product
files. Active calculator saves, accounts, migrations and lifecycle coordination
remain unchanged until their integration is separately reviewed.

## The useful first capability

A caller with an explicit application owner/access policy can persist a freshly
computed SDK envelope, list/read it, export the same envelope, and delete it from
an isolated local database. Use the real production IndexedDB API in tests as
well as deterministic failure controls. Merely defining a detached schema does
not finish this slice.

Use a new database `zodiacs-saved-natal-v1`, version 1, with one `records` store,
compound owner/record identity and an owner index. Keep this in the profile
layer: do not introduce an engine-store service, account sync protocol, general
repository framework or automatic default singleton. Importing the module must
not open storage, create a guest vault, call the network or activate UI behavior.

Reuse the existing Living Chart transaction and invalidation conventions,
including owner-key partitioning and fixed storage errors. Do not reuse its
physical database or insert natal values into its moments store. The existing
adapter explicitly opens database version 1; the native browser control proves
that adding an object store through version 2 makes that old adapter throw
`VersionError`. Mixing record types into `moments` would instead violate its
all-record parser and owner clear semantics. A separate database is necessary
for version ownership; a generic engine persistence layer is not.

Avoid changing the existing Living Chart adapter while implementing this slice.
Its short transaction pattern can guide the new domain adapter; broad extraction
of a configurable storage framework would expand review without improving this
first consumer's safety.

## Record and callable surface

Use an immutable record containing schema version, fresh cryptographic UUID,
explicit owner scope, created timestamp, optional bounded plain label, and
`envelopeJson`. No copied body projection, inputSnapshot, historical timezone
lookup or second numerical validator belongs in the stored authority.

`envelopeJson` is produced/validated by the installed SDK's
`serializeNatalEnvelope` / `parseNatalEnvelope`. Preserve unknown permitted SDK
extensions as inert data. Local metadata must not be inserted into the SDK
receipt or leaked through its export. Avoid inventing another wrapper import
format: the first import capability, if needed, accepts only the already
supported bounded SDK envelope format and treats it as claimed data, not a
verified fresh calculation.

The minimal callable operations are:

- `create(calculation, metadata?)`: create one immutable saved record with a new
  random ID. Capture and validate the envelope before awaiting storage, preserving
  the detached calculation. No request deduplication through fallback houses or
  hashes. Do not accept an existing ID or overwrite records.
- `get(id)` and `list()`: owner-bound reads; report malformed records or unavailable
  storage explicitly. Do not return an empty array on failure.
- `exportEnvelope(id)`: return the existing SDK envelope JSON only. Exclude owner,
  record ID, local label and storage timestamps. This is sensitive chart export,
  not the redacted diagnostic.
- `delete(id)`: owner-scoped, idempotent physical deletion. Immutable records and
  internally generated creation IDs avoid an update path that can resurrect a
  deleted ID.
- `revoke()` / `close()`: invalidate pending access, abort tracked active native
  transactions where still possible and reject stale completions. Do not create
  a new authority automatically.

Method names are author choices; those semantics are the reviewed contract.
Use explicit result unions distinguishing success, missing, stale/access denied,
storage unavailable, invalid record and full. Do not return raw exceptions,
payload values or causes in errors. An immutable initial record needs no unused
revision counter or updatedAt. Add metadata rename with meaningful optimistic
revision only if required, rather than adding an edit operation to justify a
counter.

Enforce the existing 40-chart cap in the same readwrite transaction as insertion.
Reject invalid owner IDs, malformed records, oversized envelopes and unusable
UUID generation without partial writes. Prefer bounded owner reads (at most the
supported cap plus one corruption sentinel) rather than an unbounded collection
API. Every local key and row must agree with its stored owner and ID.

## Authority and asynchronous boundaries

An owner callback is application access policy, not proof of authentication and
not a sandbox against hostile same-origin JavaScript. The primitive must make
that limitation explicit; it must never infer a verified account merely from a
localStorage owner marker or a caller-supplied account ID.

Bind each store/session to one captured owner. Capture a generation at operation
entry, then recheck owner/access/generation before transaction work, inside its
read-success callback, and before returning data or dispatching a success event.
A→B→A is still a different authority generation. A late `open()` must close its
handle after revocation. Capture the owner in each transaction key; never choose
an owner again after an await.

`IDBDatabase.close()` does not abort an already active transaction. Track native
transactions and call `abort()` during revocation when they are still active.
If a transaction already committed to the captured owner before revocation, the
stale caller must receive neither data nor success. A stale response must not
promise that nothing was stored and must not trigger an automatic retry.

No UI/default singleton is activated by this slice. Its next integration must
connect the existing profile read lease and auth/access epoch coordinator; the
new library must not manufacture a parallel global authority manager.

## Why another localStorage key is insufficient

The actual old aggregate writer can acknowledge two saves and retain only one
under an allowed interleaving. A new aggregate key reproduces that design flaw.
Per-record keys prevent different IDs from clobbering each other, but do not
make same-record check/write, capacity checks, export snapshots or clear/create
atomic. A version field alone is not compare-and-swap. Web Locks could serialize
cooperating writers, but require every writer and lifecycle action to adopt the
same protocol; lock-unavailable environments would need a fail-closed result.
The application already has an appropriate IndexedDB transaction pattern.

The HTML standard explicitly warns that localStorage shared-state users should
assume no locking, while IndexedDB serializes overlapping readwrite transactions.
These support the transaction choice; they do not establish application owner
authority. Sources: [Web storage](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/webstorage.html)
and [IndexedDB transaction scheduling](https://www.w3.org/TR/IndexedDB-3/#transaction-scheduling).

## Exact activation blockers and next integration

1. Existing account discovery and handoff are synchronous and enumerate five
   explicit profile keys. A newly added global key is not detected, archived or
   deleted by account-specific cleanup. The probe reproduces that with actual
   boundary functions. New database presence must join an asynchronous pre-grant
   check, or a rigorously durable presence/clear protocol; a best-effort boolean
   marker is not enough to authorize access.
2. Keep account/guest records in their owner namespaces on switch; do not copy
   all richer records into the legacy five-key archive. Revoke old access before
   the next owner can read. Guest import/ownership transfer remains an explicit
   user choice, not an inference from missing legacy charts or a matching UTC.
3. New database cleanup must participate in account deletion, sign-out removal,
   retained-data choices, per-owner cleanup and full-device cleanup. The existing
   prefix enumeration erases localStorage, not this new database. Reuse the
   durable content-free clear-request/retry convention, preserve failed clear
   intent, and prevent delayed operations from recreating data after clear.
   Do not report physical erasure merely because a clear request was queued.
4. The existing account export downloads server data. It cannot silently claim
   to include these local records. Add an explicit local envelope export action
   before active saves; preserve account export as its present remote operation.
5. Richer records must stay out of legacy sync. Current v1 sync uploads all charts
   from its old profile when the v2 flag is off. Do not dual-write authoritative
   records there. Any richer-to-v1 write preparation must return a fixed
   `requires-version-2` outcome before mutation IDs, serialization or network.
   Read-only legacy entries remain explicitly legacy; never reconstruct a full
   envelope from their abbreviated body summaries.

Those are concrete dependencies for activation, not owner-only holds on the
optional persistence primitive. The author can implement and test the callable
store now without claiming that active C02 save/rerun loss has been fixed.

## Decisive acceptance cases

- Actual portable polar Placidus and explicit Whole requests persist as distinct
  records; unknown 08:30 time remains unknown with actual houses null and its
  exact captured reference/source spelling. Envelope export/reparse preserves
  all fields and excludes local owner metadata.
- Concurrent distinct creates retain both; concurrent cap-bound creates cannot
  exceed 40. A missing record differs from unavailable/corrupt storage.
- Revocation before open, during read, inside a write callback and before commit
  fails closed. A→B→A cannot return A's earlier data. Separate owner IDs with the
  same record ID never cross. An already committed stale operation is described
  honestly without an automatic retry.
- Corrupted rows, cross-owner row/key disagreement, unsupported SDK schemas or
  required features, oversized JSON, invalid metadata and storage exceptions
  produce bounded errors and no data-bearing notifications.
- A failed insertion transaction writes nothing; successful completion is awaited.
  Same-owner immutable deletion is idempotent and cannot target another owner.
- Both owner read-only and write-capable policies are tested if the API supports
  that distinction. Optional graph checks prove no new load from existing
  engine/full/math/UI paths, and no storage/network on import.
- The subsequent lifecycle integration—not this primitive—must demonstrate guest
  discovery/import, account switch/retention/archive, clear/retry, export and
  deletion against the actual coordinator, including cross-tab/native browser
  schedules. Do not label those active flows passed from injection-only tests.
